  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "No apparent truncation or garbling; the full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper studies interpretability of routed reward-model experts, not individual neurons or neuron poles, and does not test the listed neuron-specific versions.",
    "gate_note": "The measured units are MoE reward-model experts (linear reward heads and their routing patterns), not neurons in language models. The expert results are recorded as related findings with versions [\"none\"]; they do not establish whether neuron explanations generalize to unseen activations. The paper also reports personalization and policy-training results, which do not measure the neuron claim.",
    "test_reason": "The study audits routed linear-head experts in MoE reward models (a router over a frozen backbone), not neurons or neuron poles in language or vision models. It neither measures neuron job shares nor scores neuron explanations by activation simulation. Its expert-description fidelity test resembles V6/V7 methodologically but concerns a different unit (reward experts trained with sparsity regularizers), so it is recorded under fits_no_version. It mentions sparsity and superposition only as related work.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that conventional MoE reward models learn entangled experts, and that this is a property of the training objective, not the architecture. Sparsity and diversity regularizers yield coherent, specialized experts. Routing interventions confirm the experts' roles, and the experts compose for few-shot personalization, at a modest cost in universal accuracy.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper trains and evaluates MoE reward-model experts with routing regularizers; it does not use the neuron-pole auditing or held-out neuron-explanation methods in the claim sources.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Expert count changes the reported balance between description fidelity and specialization: fidelity rises through K=30 and then is relatively stable, while specialization decreases as K increases. Regularizer weights also affect routing collapse and specialization.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On SHP, sparse MoE reward experts reached 84.76% purity and 5.56 specialization, against 60.80/-0.43 for vanilla MoE and 50.27/0.24 for MiCRo.",
        "proposed_claim": "Sparsity and diversity regularizers make MoE reward-model experts more coherent and more functionally specialized.",
        "row_ids": [
          "shp-vanilla-purity",
          "shp-micro-purity",
          "shp-sparse-purity",
          "shp-vanilla-specialization",
          "shp-micro-specialization",
          "shp-sparse-specialization"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Descriptions written from top-routed examples correlate only modestly with routing weights on held-out examples (sparse MoE 0.38, vanilla 0.29, MiCRo 0.15 on 700K; one expert scores -0.19). This is analogous to held-out explanation scoring, but for experts.",
        "proposed_claim": "Explanations of reward-model experts drawn from top-activating examples only partly predict routing on new inputs, even under interpretability regularization.",
        "row_ids": [
          "700k-vanilla-fidelity",
          "700k-micro-fidelity",
          "700k-sparse-fidelity",
          "700k-vanilla-specialization",
          "700k-micro-specialization",
          "700k-sparse-specialization"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "The same pattern holds with a Qwen3-8B backbone (sparse fidelity 0.41 vs 0.01/-0.00) and on LMArena (0.23 vs 0.08/0.07).",
        "proposed_claim": "The expert-level interpretability gains from regularization transfer across reward-model backbones and preference datasets.",
        "row_ids": [
